There will be a Open Systems World/FedUnix conference/trade show in
Washington DC on November 4-8.  I am chairing a Linux-related session
at this conference (scheduled for Thursday, November 7, 1996) and I am
looking for speakers at this session.

This conference typically attracts customers from federal government
and related contractors. Before you say 'boring', consider that
those include research community (NASA, other federal laboratories),
communication folks (satellite, telephone, etc.), medical research
(National Institutes of Health), and many other interesting areas.

An ideal talk would present your experiences using Linux in an
interesting and novel situation. Have you replaced a mainframe with a
Linux server? Have you written or ported a large, scientifically or
commercially important program on Linux? Have you bet a
mission-critical application on the only peer-reviewed OS?

We are primarily interested in technical issues (good and bad aspects
of Linux development), but also in long-term plans regarding Linux. I
hope my session will allow people with interesting stories to spread
the word to a government and industry technical audience that
appreciates Linux.

I imagine a 20-minute, not-terribly-formal presentations.  
I would like to firm up the schedule by mid-September. Please
send me a note with the title and short, one-paragraph abstract of
your talk, by September 5. I won't be able to respond before the
end of August, but I will contact everyone by mid-September.

We will of course waive conference fees for the speakers, and we may
be able to help with travel cost in individual cases (cash-starved
students and such).

