-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Comp.os.linux.announce has been working rather badly for a while, due
to various personal problems. Things are improving. I have just cleared
the incoming queue, and answered all questions I found in my inbox. If
you haven't received a note about your announcement or a reply to your
questions, please send it again.

I would also like to take this opportunity to say a few other things.
This is a bit long, but please read it through.

First, I've grown tired of e-mail spam. I've developed a mail filter
to stop it, but it has the unfortunate drawback that it makes it more
difficult to reach me. Read

	http://www.iki.fi/liw/mail-to-lasu.html
	
before sending me e-mail. Reading it isn't necessary, but it saves an
extra, day-long round trip. Essentially, my filter deletes mail that does
not come from someone I know, or that doesn't have a special password
in the subject.

The anti-spam filter does not apply to comp.os.linux.announce submissions,
but you need to be aware of it if you need to ask me something.

(Actually, the filter isn't fully active yet -- postings aren't deleted,
just put in a separate folder. But read the page anyway.)

Second, I don't have time to read most of the Linux newsgroups. They seem
to be full of questions. I don't know if it is needed, but those who read
them actively might want to look at

	http://www.boutell.com/boutell/usenet.html
	
and think about whether it would be a good thing for comp.os.linux.* (not
including .announce and .answers).

Third, I notice that the quality of Linux advocacy is rather low. I'm
not overly concerned about this, but it _might_ lead to the Amiga syndrome
(an Amiga user will claim that Amiga is the best computer system ever, for
any purpose, in any discussion of computers). I wrote down my thoughts
about this, see

	http://www.iki.fi/liw/texts/advocating-linux.html
	
Comments welcome. It is not inspired by any recent events -- the first
draft is from, I think, March.

Fourth, I've put together some notes for people who wish to post to
c.o.l.a.  See the official web page

	http://www.iki.fi/liw/linux/cola.html
	
The posting guide is still just a draft, and I would like to see comments,
especially from readers. Is there something you'd like announcers to
do to make it easier for you use c.o.l.a? (Remember, c.o.l.a exists for
its readers.)

Comments are also welcome on the c.o.l.a web page in general, except for
complaints about not having a search engine. That will come one of these
days, when I have some time to start hacking on it. There's rather extensive
indexes, however.

Fifth, at the moment hardware advertisments are essentially banned from
this group. I've thought about allowing them if they fill the following
criteria:

         a) only the manufacturer posts, resellers are not welcome (this 
	 	already applies to all commercial posts)
         b) the system comes with Linux installed, or with a Linux
                 distribution bundled, or has some other reason why
                 it is of special interest for the group ("nice hardware"
                 is not enough; "nice hardware with Linux" might be)
         c) the posts don't get too frequent (read: I don't want every
                 hole-in-the-wall seller that assembles his own machines
                 to start posting, since in Helsinki, Finland alone there
                 would be a dozen or so of them)

I discussed this with someone in March, but haven't done anything about
it yet. Tell me what you think. (Mail me, but read the mail-to-lasu.html
link first.)

Sixth, I'm still getting submissions that are sent via a mailing list
or using a Bcc. Don't do that. My mail filter doesn't sort them properly
(they are treated as spam), and I won't process them for c.o.l.a. Post via
news or mention the official address in To or Cc.

Seventh, I'm going to start using PGP Moose for article authentication,
although not immediately. Do not be alarmed if you start seeing articles
without my PGP signature. PGP Moose is specially designed for moderated
groups, and the signature is moved to the headers. You need special
software to validate the signature, but on the other hand, there will
be less user-visible junk in the articles. For more information on
PGP Moose, see

	http://www.usenix.org/~ggr/pgpmoose.html

Incidentally, PGP Moose has a server that needs to be running to check
the articles (to guard against forged approvals). At the moment, there
are not enough servers and it would be good to have many. If you are
running a news server and can handle a little extra load, and would be
willing to run the PGP Moose server, please volunteer. It's not just for
c.o.l.a, several moderated groups are already using PGP Moose. This is
part of a larger campaign to prevent net abuse.

Thanks for reading all this. Sorry to be so boring. Please accept
the following as an apology:

	"I have often had the impression that, to penguins, man is just
	another penguin---different, less predictable, occasionally
	violent, but tolerable company when he sits still and minds his
	own business."	-- Bernard Stonehouse

(from "Bloom County Babylon---Five years of basic naughtiness", by
Berke Breathed).

That should be an appropriate quote, given Linux's new logo.

