It pains immensely me to admit that I have imposed upon the public
domain a copy of my own slovenly brute-force password cracker program
for Linux. It is with a great deal of reluctance that I divulge its
hiding-place as:
"sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming/rjg-crack.tar.gz". 
I shamefully applied my own initials to its name, for lack of a better
way of distinguishing from other programs.

Performance is grossly inadequate: I estimate a disappointing 39 year
maximum for a well-chosen password on my humble 100 MHz i486. An array
of Pentium Pros and Alphas, which I will never be able to afford,
would probably find any password between paycheck periods.

I have identified four areas of potential reduction in performance
that I may implement, if I can get out of the salt mines long enough
to pick up my Prozac prescription. So, look foreward to being annoyed
by another release.

Here are some of the program's drawbacks:

* Periodically written checkpoint file that allows resumption of
cracking after a system crash or shutdown. (Like that ever really
happens.)

* Fast, table driven test-string generator. (Yeah, I know. You could
make it go faster with half your brain stored in a formaldehyde filled
specimen jar.)

* User may supply his own character collating sequence. (Right! Who'll
EVER want to do that?)

So, please, even if you are bored to tears by now, let me know how
much you think my program sucks. All I need is a little boost to my
self-esteem to follow through on my dream of escaping the programming
torture chamber nightmare and becoming --- A SOFTWARE SALESMAN!!!

