-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

LOGTIME 1.10 - Tracks usage time/cost when connected via SLIP/PPP
Copyright (c) 1996 by Christopher S L Heng. All rights reserved.
- ----------------------------------------------------------------

$Id: cola.ann 1.5 1996/07/28 22:51:01 chris Exp $ /*


1. What is LOGTIME?

LOGTIME allows users to keep track of the amount of time and charges
accumulated when logged into an Internet Service Provider via SLIP/PPP.
It is able to differentiate between peak and non-peak hours if required.
LOGTIME also allows users to maintain one set of log files for
connections made in Linux and in Windows (both Linux and Windows
versions are included).


2. What's New in Version 1.10

* The Linux version of LOGTIME now has installation and deinstallation
scripts

* Both ELF and a.out binaries of LOGTIME are now included

* The verbose option of the Linux version of LOGTIME is now slightly
more informative

* The Windows version of LOGTIME now looks for its help file in the
directory from which it was run regardless of the data directory.
Previously, if you changed the data directory, it would look for the
help file in the data directory. This change of behaviour makes it
easier to use LOGTIME with multiple configurations and data files
(which is useful if you are connected to more than one ISP).

* Minor bug fixes (does not affect time-keeping accuracy)

* Updated and reorganised documentation

For those of you who requested new features: sorry, most of them are
not implemented in this version. This is mainly an interim version to
include both ELF and a.out binaries; the previous version only had the
a.out binary.


3. Some Features

LOGTIME has the following features:

- - It keeps track of time by being called from your login and logout
script in your dial-up program.

- - Some ISPs charge different rates for connections made during the day
and those in the night and/or connections made on a public holiday or
weekend and those on a working day. LOGTIME is able to distinguish
between such connections and display the appropriate results if
configured correctly.

- - It displays the amount of time you have spent as well as the charges
you have accumulated so far. If your ISP provides you with a block of
time which you have prepaid, LOGTIME will display the amount of time
that you have left for that month (as compared to the prepaid time) or
the amount of time in excess of the prepaid time that you have used.

- - The log file, which contains the record of login/logout times, and the
configuration file are compatible between the versions of LOGTIME for
Windows and Linux. You can keep the log and configuration files in a
place accessible by the LOGTIME for the two systems, and use one set of
files for accesses made in both systems!

- - LOGTIME is configurable: you can change the charges it is to calculate
for the time you clock, you can modify the way LOGTIME rounds the time
clocked, you can set the days which it is to consider as public
holidays, or change the times which it is to consider as working hours.


4. Where to get it?

I have uploaded LOGTIME to

	ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/Incoming/logtime-1.10.tar.gz

It will probably be moved to

	ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/system/Serial/logtime-1.10.tar.gz

This file includes both the Linux and Windows binaries and complete online
manuals for both systems (no sources). LOGTIME is copyrighted but free.

LOGTIME is also available from the following locations:
	ftp://ftp.simtel.net/pub/simtelnet/win3/telecomm/lt110.zip
	ftp://ftp.winsite.com/pub/win3/winsock/lt110.zip

The contents of the zipped archives are identical with those of the
tarred archive.


5. Contacting the author

You can contact me at

	ChristopherHeng@pobox.org.sg (Christopher Heng)
	cyfheng@singnet.com.sg (Christopher Heng)


